WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:17.949
SOFTWARE ENGINEER COUNT:
At the end of 2021, Twitter had a total of

00:00:17.949 --> 00:00:19.580
7,500 employees.

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:24.640
After acquiring Twitter, Elon Musk would viciously
lay off the workforce shrinking it to just

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:30.080
1,300 employees, out of which only 550 were
full-time engineers.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:35.300
With such a purge, you would think that outages
become commonplace, bugs and glitches become

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:39.879
a normality, and the infrastructure of the
company collapses from within.

00:00:39.879 --> 00:00:44.629
But, as far as we can tell, Twitter is still
completely operational.

00:00:44.629 --> 00:00:49.629
I mean as an average user, you wouldn’t
even know that such a massive change unfolded

00:00:49.629 --> 00:00:50.850
on the backend.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:54.690
Not only has Twitter remained operational,
but they’ve even been able to roll out some

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:57.010
new features like Twitter Blue.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:59.430
But, this doesn't make any sense.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:04.671
If 1300 employees are able to keep Twitter
functional and even launch new features, why

00:01:04.671 --> 00:01:09.460
in the world did Twitter have 7,500 employees
just 6 months ago?

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:14.620
This might seem like a Twitter specific question
at first glance, but when you zoom out you’ll

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:18.940
realize that the same question applies to
virtually every tech company.

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:22.410
Facebook had a total of 86,000 employees.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:25.840
Google had a total of 190,000 employees.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:29.480
And Apple had a total of 164,000 employees.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:34.730
Now of course, not all of these employees
are software engineers but a substantial portion

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:38.030
of them are as in 30-50%.

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:44.170
And even more of them are tech workers involved
in program management, IT, and solutions architecting.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:47.860
It’s not just these big FAANG companies
either.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.610
The same question applies to well known startups
as well.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.850
Robinhod 3,800 employees.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:55.260
AirBnB, 6,800 employees.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:57.670
Uber, 33,000 employees.

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:02.880
I mean, why in the world does Uber need 33,000
employees.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.149
This becomes even more confusing when you
consider that most of the core services for

00:02:07.149 --> 00:02:12.090
all of these companies are already built out
and functional for years if not decades.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:16.819
I mean, modern software engineers at Google
aren’t going to be writing search algorithms

00:02:16.819 --> 00:02:22.020
from scratch, and software engineers at Instagram
aren’t going to be creating messaging infrastructure

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:23.020
from scratch.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:27.610
Now of course, many of these software engineers
have been getting laid off in recent times.

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:29.710
Google laid of 12,000 people.

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:32.280
Facebook has laid of 21,000 people.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:34.660
And you know all those startups that we were
talking about?

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:40.260
Well, most of them have laid off 40-50% of
their workforce, but this simply furthers

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:45.709
the narrative that these companies are able
to get away with far smaller workforces . So,

00:02:45.709 --> 00:02:50.080
why exactly did they have so many software
engineers in the first place and what exactly

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.930
were all of them even doing?

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:58.360
THE OVERHIRING CRISIS:
Common sense would lead you to believe that

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:03.760
running these companies simply requires such
a large workforce, but that’s not exactly

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:04.760
true.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:09.390
While coding may seem intimidating and difficult
from an outside perspective, the truth is

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:13.140
that most coding projects are actually pretty
straight forward.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:17.540
Creating an app like Instagram or AirBnB isn’t
actually that hard.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:22.769
AirBnB for example could be created by a single
developer and a single UX designer within

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:24.010
16 weeks.

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:28.540
And, you don’t even have to outsource the
labor to cheap developers overseas.

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:34.750
You can pay the developer $5,000 every week
and the UX designer $50 per hour, and it would

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:36.860
only cost you $82,000.

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:39.739
But, you don’t even have to look that far.

00:03:39.739 --> 00:03:41.600
Instead, you can look back.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:46.099
Many of the tech apps and services that we
use on a daily basis were initially completely

00:03:46.099 --> 00:03:48.510
developed by less than 5 people.

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:52.879
The initial WhatsApp was created by a Russian
developer from RentACoder.com.

00:03:52.879 --> 00:03:56.050
The initial AirBnB website was created by
three roommates.

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:58.500
The initial Uber App was created by 3 friends.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:03.329
And the initial version of Twitter was created
by just Dorsey and a contractor.

00:04:03.329 --> 00:04:07.870
Now of course, as you grow, you wouldn’t
want to rely on less than 5 developers.

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:12.659
You would want developers checking each others
work, developers who are focused on user retention,

00:04:12.659 --> 00:04:16.840
developers who are focused on onboarding,
developers who are focused on A/B testing,

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:19.120
developers who are focused on backend engineering,
and so on.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.530
You would also want program managers who are
leading each of these efforts and a CTO who

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:25.770
can set company wide goals and timelines.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:29.509
But, none of this takes tens of thousands
of employees.

00:04:29.509 --> 00:04:35.039
More realistically, it would take hundreds
of employees and if you wanna be generous,

00:04:35.039 --> 00:04:39.389
you can call it a 1000 employees similar to
what Twitter has right now.

00:04:39.389 --> 00:04:44.750
Most companies did indeed start off this way,
but somewhere along the line, they started

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:46.940
hiring far past their needs.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:49.030
Take Facebook for example.

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:52.280
At the end of 2014, they had a total of 9,200
employees.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:57.600
By this point, Facebook had already been around
for over 10 years and they owned all three

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.390
of their main platforms, Facebook, WhatsApp,
and Instagram.

00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:06.389
The core functionality of these services were
complete, and the only tasks ahead were optimization

00:05:06.389 --> 00:05:07.430
and scaling.

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:12.430
This isn’t to say that these tasks are easy,
but it’s also questionable if it warranted

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:16.789
a 9X increase in head count over the next
several years.

00:05:16.789 --> 00:05:19.440
A similar story can be seen at Google as well.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:23.930
Back in 2014, Google had a total of 54,000
employees.

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:28.500
By this point, Google already had all of their
money making services setup and performing

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:32.850
well and they had a market cap as high as
$400 billion.

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:37.520
You would think that Google could cut back
on the hiring and simply let time do it’s

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:43.120
magic, but instead Google would go ahead and
increase their head count to nearly 200,000.

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:50.250
So, if it’s not an intrinsic need that’s
pushing these companies to hire more, what

00:05:50.250 --> 00:05:51.350
is?

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:55.610
FILTHY RICH:
One of the main reasons that these companies

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:59.669
hire so many people is simply because they
can.

00:05:59.669 --> 00:06:03.590
This might sound a bit anticlimactic, but
it’s true.

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:07.710
Virtually all of the companies that we’ve
discussed so far are extraordinarily profitable

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:11.300
on their own or they have infinite venture
capital money.

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:14.930
At first these companies do the sensible thing
with all this money.

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:19.389
They build up cash reserves, they diversify
into new businesses, and they solidify their

00:06:19.389 --> 00:06:21.250
user base and brand image.

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:24.710
But, eventually they run out of things to
do.

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:26.720
They simply reach market saturation.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:31.770
Traditionally, this is when a company would
chill out and pay dividends, but these are

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:34.050
tech companies that we’re talking about.

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:39.610
Traditional oil, consumer, and industrial
giants take 50, 70, 100 years to get to this

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:40.610
point.

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:43.949
So, the transition to dividends feels more
natural.

00:06:43.949 --> 00:06:47.889
These tech companies, however, often get to
this point within 5, 10, 20 years, and they’re

00:06:47.889 --> 00:06:49.199
used to ludicrous amounts of growth which
has warped their perspective quite a bit.

00:06:49.199 --> 00:06:54.710
Pretty soon, this leads to some pretty weird
hiring habits like hiring with no intention.

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:58.550
This might sound confusing, but this is literally
what happens.

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:03.520
At a normal company, if your team needs a
new person, your manager would probably put

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:05.199
in a request with HR.

00:07:05.199 --> 00:07:09.630
HR would then turn around and put up a job
posting and leave it up for a months or so

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:12.080
to attract a sufficient number of candidates.

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:16.389
From there, HR would pass on the most promising
candidates to your manager who would then

00:07:16.389 --> 00:07:19.129
interview these people and choose the best
one.

00:07:19.129 --> 00:07:22.240
This is how a normal sensible company hires.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:26.050
But, at most tech companies, it’s the exact
opposite.

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:30.680
In fact, the needs of specific teams is mostly
irrelevant.

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:35.370
Instead, these companies have generic job
listings like software engineer and program

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:37.500
manager that are up forever.

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:43.320
And it’s not until candidates pass the arduous
hiring bar that Google even thinks about where

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:44.320
to place them.

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:48.680
This often results in candidates passing the
hiring bar, but then getting infinitely stuck

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:51.030
in the team matching phase.

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:52.590
Why does Google do this you ask?

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:57.330
Well, their philosophy is basically, we have
a crap ton a money, so let’s just hire the

00:07:57.330 --> 00:08:01.610
best people and we can worry about giving
them something to do later.

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:07.830
For obvious reasons, this leads to a pretty
bloated workforce and team switching policies

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:09.720
just make this even worse.

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:13.669
In traditional companies, it’s relatively
hard to switch teams.

00:08:13.669 --> 00:08:17.860
The reason for this is that you were hired
to do a specific job, so if you switch to

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:22.139
another team, they now have to find someone
to do your old job.

00:08:22.139 --> 00:08:26.870
At many tech companies though, you weren’t
hired to do a job, you were simply hired based

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:28.840
on your skills and general knowledge.

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:34.500
So, they have some pretty lenient team switching
policies which candidates naturally tend to

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:35.500
leverage.

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:39.620
You see, over time, candidates have come to
realize that if they waste too much time in

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:45.019
team matching, they might not get a job at
the company despite passing the hiring bar.

00:08:45.019 --> 00:08:49.589
So, it’s in their best interest to join
the first team they can and just switch to

00:08:49.589 --> 00:08:53.070
the team they really want 6 or 12 months down
the line.

00:08:53.070 --> 00:08:57.329
While this is clever, this leads to a two
fold issue.

00:08:57.329 --> 00:09:01.430
First of all, it’s not clear how useful
they will be during the first 6-12 months

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:02.700
in the first team.

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:07.339
And secondly, it’s likely that most Google
and Facebook hopefuls have the same dream

00:09:07.339 --> 00:09:11.339
teams in mind leading to certain teams becoming
even more bloated.

00:09:11.339 --> 00:09:17.041
But, despite all this, these companies will
still continue hiring because they’re chasing

00:09:17.041 --> 00:09:18.829
infinite growth.

00:09:18.829 --> 00:09:24.370
CHASING INFINITE GROWTH:
Why do tech companies have so many employees?

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:30.000
Well, the answer is that they not only overhire
but most of these employees end up working

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.770
on things that will never see the light of
day.

00:09:32.770 --> 00:09:36.390
Things like weather balloons that are supposed
to increase internet connectivity.

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:38.870
Underwater cameras for ocean farmers.

00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:42.079
AI that’s supposed to increase agriculture.

00:09:42.079 --> 00:09:44.620
Light that can enable wireless connections.

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:50.230
These are all projects that Google is working
on right now and those are just a few of the

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:51.399
public ones.

00:09:51.399 --> 00:09:54.970
Who knows how many more projects Google has
behind the scenes.

00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:59.550
And as for Facebook, do I even have to say
anything about the Metaverse?

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:02.290
The Metaverse is just one example though.

00:10:02.290 --> 00:10:07.370
Several years ago, Facebook was pouring substantial
resources into building drone internet.

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:09.579
And who could forget Google glass?

00:10:09.579 --> 00:10:15.839
The reality is that most of these moonshot
projects don’t end up going anywhere, but

00:10:15.839 --> 00:10:18.950
end up requiring a lot of employees and resources.

00:10:18.950 --> 00:10:21.840
This doesn’t just apply to moonshot ideas
either.

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:26.769
Most projects relating to even successful
projects like Chrome and YouTube don’t generate

00:10:26.769 --> 00:10:28.279
any ROI.

00:10:28.279 --> 00:10:33.209
For example, if you use chrome, you may have
noticed that Google has recently been experimenting

00:10:33.209 --> 00:10:35.580
the UI of downloads.

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:39.560
Instead of having a download shelf on the
bottom, they are experimenting with a download

00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:40.560
icon on the top right.

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:44.730
This may indeed be better, but it’s hard
to say if it will actually increase Google’s

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:47.660
revenue or profit in any meaningful way.

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:52.060
It probably did, however, keep several engineers
busy for several weeks.

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:57.930
But, if the ROI isn’t there, why do these
companies continue pursuing these projects?

00:10:57.930 --> 00:11:01.329
Well, the answer is that they’re chasing
endless growth.

00:11:01.329 --> 00:11:06.810
They’ve exhausted most of the obvious avenues,
so all they can do now is experiment with

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:11.149
out there ideas and optimize the UI to the
absolute max.

00:11:11.149 --> 00:11:16.339
Most users will never spot these changes or
use these new products or make the company

00:11:16.339 --> 00:11:20.930
any extra money, but that doesn’t mean that
these companies will stop trying.

00:11:20.930 --> 00:11:25.959
After all, the end of the quarter is always
around the corner, and they want to please

00:11:25.959 --> 00:11:26.970
shareholders.

00:11:26.970 --> 00:11:33.290
THE STATE OF TECH:
In the end, the harsh reality is that most

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:37.670
tech companies don’t need anywhere near
as many employees as they have.

00:11:37.670 --> 00:11:42.000
If they were just trying to maintain their
platforms, they could get away with 20-30%

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:43.980
of their workforce if not les.

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:47.529
But, they’re not just trying to maintain
their platforms, they’re trying to add another

00:11:47.529 --> 00:11:50.930
ten billion or another trillion onto their
marketcap.

00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:56.160
And they’re willing to over hire tens of
thousands of employees, burn billions of dollars,

00:11:56.160 --> 00:12:00.160
and waste tens of millions of man hours in
the hopes that maybe they’ll find the next

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:03.370
Chrome or the next iPhone or the next Instagram.

00:12:03.370 --> 00:12:06.389
So far, this hasn’t exactly worked out.

00:12:06.389 --> 00:12:09.230
Some parts of the Metaverse have 38 users.

00:12:09.230 --> 00:12:15.060
We have yet to see the Apple car or the Apple
VR headset or the Apple AR glasses or whatever.

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:20.889
And Google’s most successful moonshot idea,
Waymo, is still somehow eons behind Tesla.

00:12:20.889 --> 00:12:23.070
None of this mattered though.

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:28.880
They had money and they were gonna try to
brute force creativity, innovation, and a

00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:29.880
market need.

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:34.779
But, for the first time, these companies are
starting to see their money go down and these

00:12:34.779 --> 00:12:37.500
projects take a toll on the core business.

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:41.470
And it’s only now that these companies are
starting to realize that they’re inside

00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:43.680
an overhiring nightmare.

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:47.670
Doesn’t really matter for the companies
though as it’s the employees that have to

00:12:47.670 --> 00:12:53.389
deal with a 20, 30, 40, 50% reductions in
the workforce and that’s the harsh reality

00:12:53.389 --> 00:12:55.149
of the tech industry right now.

00:12:55.149 --> 00:12:57.990
Do you think overhiring is reasonable?

00:12:57.990 --> 00:12:59.330
Comment that down below.

00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:04.769
Also, drop a like if wish that employees weren’t
the ones getting the short end of the stick.

00:13:04.769 --> 00:13:09.000
And of course consider checking our discord
community to suggest future video ideas and

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:15.790
consider subscribing to see more questions
logically answered.

